<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1710 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">O Come, O Come, Emmanuel</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="la">Veni, veni, Emmanuel</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A hymn for the weeks before Christmas. Its Latin words were printed in 1710 and its tune was found in a book from the 1400s.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (Latin); English translation by John Mason Neale</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (15th-century French processional melody, known as &quot;Veni Emmanuel&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Catholic Germany, where the Latin hymn was first printed; later the Church of England and English-speaking churches</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Cologne, Germany (first printing of the Latin words)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>Latin words first printed 1710; tune in a 15th-century manuscript; words and tune first joined in 1851</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous Latin words first printed in 1710; English translation by John Mason Neale (1851, revised 1861); the tune is from a 15th-century French manuscript)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-come-o-come-emmanuel">O Come, O Come, Emmanuel</a>&quot;, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> &quot;Veni, veni, Emmanuel&quot;, is a Christian hymn for Advent, the season before Christmas, and is often printed among Christmas carols. Its words are a rhymed paraphrase of the O Antiphons, short chants sung with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a> at Vespers on the last days before Christmas. The antiphons go back to monastic life of the 8th or 9th century, and that has led to a common belief that the hymn is just as old; claims that it dates from the 11th or 12th century are often repeated. But the Latin hymn first appears in print in 1710, in the seventh edition of the Psalteriolum Cantionum Catholicarum, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. That hymnal, first assembled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Johannes Heringsdorf in 1610 and used widely in Jesuit schools, was a major force in German church music.</p><p>The 1710 hymn has five verses, each paraphrasing one antiphon, with a two-line refrain of rejoicing; two antiphons are left out, and the antiphon of Emmanuel is moved to the front, which gave the hymn its name. In 1844 the German hymnologist Hermann Adalbert Daniel included it in the second volume of his Thesaurus Hymnologicus. A seven-verse form, with two added verses of unknown authorship for the missing antiphons, was printed in Joseph Hermann Mohr's Cantiones Sacrae in 1878.</p><p>The English hymn grew from Daniel's collection. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-mason-connecticut">John Mason</a> Neale published the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in his Hymni Ecclesiae of 1851, and in the same year the first known English translation, in Mediæval Hymns and Sequences, under a different first line. He revised it for The Hymnal Noted and again, in 1861, for Hymns Ancient and Modern, where it took the first line by which it is known. Thomas Alexander Lacey made a new translation for The English Hymnal in 1906, Henry Sloane Coffin translated the two extra verses in 1916, and a full seven-verse English version appeared in the Episcopal Church's hymnal of 1940.</p><p>The words and the tune grew up apart. Thomas Helmore first joined them in The Hymnal Noted in 1851, naming his source as &quot;a French Missal in the National Library, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>&quot;; he gave no way to check it, and some suspected that he had written the melody himself. In 1966 the British musicologist Mary Berry, an Augustinian canoness writing as Mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-more">Thomas More</a>, found the melody in a 15th-century French processional of chants for burials in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheque-nationale-de-france">National Library of France</a>, where it carries another text, &quot;Bone Jesu dulcis cunctis&quot;, as part of a series of tropes. No evidence links the tune to the hymn before Helmore, so the two first came together in English.</p><p>The pairing suited its moment. The Hymnal Noted, made up entirely of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hymns in English with music drawn mainly from plainchant, was a product of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford-movement">Oxford Movement</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, and the hymn was taken up for its medieval air and its fit to the season. Hymns Ancient and Modern, which was used in three-quarters of English churches by 1895, carried it everywhere, and its 1861 form remains the usual one in the English-speaking world. In German-speaking lands, where the text began, other tunes were sung with it for many years: one with a paraphrase attributed to Christoph Bernhard Verspoell, popular in the Diocese of Münster, and one from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> (1836) with Heinrich Bone's translation, which Johann Baptist Singenberger took to Catholic communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Some American Lutheran hymnals sing it to Dmitry Bortniansky's tune &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of longing for the coming of the Messiah. Each verse calls on him by one of the titles of the ancient Advent antiphons and asks him to come, and each ends in a refrain of rejoicing that he will be born. The mood moves from yearning to joy.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Veni, veni Emmanuel!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, come, Emmanuel!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Captivum solve Israel!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Set captive Israel free,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Qui gemit in exilio,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>which groans in exile,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Privatus Dei Filio.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>deprived of the Son of God.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Gaude, gaude, Emmanuel</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rejoice, rejoice! Emmanuel</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">nascetur pro te, Israel.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>will be born for you, Israel.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Veni o Jesse virgula!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, O little rod of Jesse!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ex hostis tuos ungula,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the enemy's claw your own,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">De specu tuos tartari</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the cave of Tartarus your own</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Educ, et antro barathri.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>lead out, and from the pit of the abyss.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Veni, veni o Oriens!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, come, O Rising Sun!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Solare nos adveniens,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Comfort us by your coming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Noctis depelle nebulas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>drive away the mists of night</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dirasque noctis tenebras.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the dreadful darkness of night.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Veni clavis Davidica!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, Key of David!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Regna reclude coelica,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Open wide the heavenly kingdoms,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Fac iter Tutum superum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make safe the road of those above,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Et claude vias Inferum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and close the ways of those below.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Veni, veni Adonai!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, come, Adonai,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Qui populo in Sinai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who to the people on Sinai</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Legem dedisti vertice,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>gave the law from the summit,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">In maiestate gloriae.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in the majesty of glory.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous; the Latin text of the Psalteriolum Cantionum Catholicarum (1710), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_Come%2C_O_Come%2C_Emmanuel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>1.3: 'qui gemit' refers to Israel ('which groans').</li><li>3.1: 'virgula', a little rod or shoot (Isaiah 11:1).</li><li>3.2-3.4: one sentence: 'lead your own out from the enemy's claw, from the cave of Tartarus, and from the pit of the abyss'; 'Ex hostis tuos ungula' is translated as printed.</li><li>4.1: 'Oriens', the rising (sun), the dawn; also rendered 'Dayspring'.</li><li>5.3-5.4: 'superum' and 'inferum' are genitive plurals: the road of those above (heaven) and the ways of those below (the underworld).</li><li>6.1: Adonai, Hebrew 'Lord', kept as a name.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
