<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Translation by John Mason Neale, in Mediæval Hymns and Sequences</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1851</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation, revised 1851 and 1861</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Translation by Thomas Alexander Lacey, in The English Hymnal</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1906</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Additional verses by Henry Sloane Coffin</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1916</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation of the two later verses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Seven-verse English version, Hymnal of the Episcopal Church</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1940</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">German paraphrase attributed to Christoph Bernhard Verspoell</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 18th or early 19th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">paraphrase, with its own tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">German translation by Heinrich Bone</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation, sung to several tunes</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Trittico Botticelliano, &quot;The Gift of the Magi&quot; (Ottorino Respighi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1927</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quotes the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Adventi ének (Zoltán Kodály)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1943</span><span class="sg-chain-h">choral work on the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Concerto da Chiesa (George Dyson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1949</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first movement based on the theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sonata for Trombone and Piano (John Davison)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1957</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quotes the melody in the third movement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Die natali, Op. 37 (Samuel Barber)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quotes the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Veni, Veni, Emmanuel, percussion concerto (James MacMillan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1991</span><span class="sg-chain-h">concerto based on the hymn</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">White as Snow (U2)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2009</span><span class="sg-chain-h">takes its tune from the hymn</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Songs for Christmas (Sufjan Stevens)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2006</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Winter Carols (Blackmore's Night)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2006</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">And Winter Came... (Enya)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2008</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording in English and Latin</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Midwinter Night's Dream (Loreena McKennitt)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2008</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Wrapped in Red (Kelly Clarkson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2013</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Christmas Songs (Bad Religion)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2013</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O Antiphons</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">8th or 9th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the Latin words paraphrase them</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bone Jesu dulcis cunctis (15th-century French processional)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">15th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the melody, later named Veni Emmanuel</span></li></ul></section>
